  "aps_info": {
    "overview":"APS (Akademische Prüfstelle) is a certificate from the German embassy/cultural institute verifying authenticity and university-entrance eligibility of academic credentials — historically required for China, India, Vietnam and a few other countries before uni-assist/universities process applications.",
    "bangladesh_status":"CONFLICTING signals. Majority of sources indicate Bangladesh is NOT on the mandatory APS list (unlike India/China/Vietnam). One lower-quality aggregator claims it IS required — this conflicts with most other sources and could not be independently confirmed.",
    "recommendation":"VERIFY DIRECTLY with the German Embassy Dhaka or aps-india.de's official country list before finalizing your document workflow — this single fact changes your timeline and cost significantly. If NOT required, you instead need: (1) Bangladesh Ministry of Education attestation, (2) Bangladesh Ministry of Foreign Affairs countersignature, (3) possibly German Embassy/Mission Dhaka authentication.",
    "nsu_accreditation":"GOOD NEWS: North South University is listed in Germany's official anabin database with 'H+' status — meaning NSU degrees are recognized as equivalent/accredited by German institutions. This removes a major potential hurdle.",
    "cost_estimate":"If APS applies (unconfirmed for Bangladesh): commonly EUR 150–225 for India/China applicants — may not apply to Bangladesh given likely non-APS status.",
    "studienkolleg_note":"Not applicable — this only matters for HSC-only applicants; you already hold a completed BSc."
  },
  "gpa_conversion_info": {
    "candidate_gpa":"2.8/4.0 (NSU, EEE)",
    "german_scale_approx":"Using the modified Bavarian formula, 2.8/4.0 roughly converts to ~2.6–2.9 on the German 1.0(best)–4.0(worst) scale — weaker end of 'befriedigend' (satisfactory), bordering 'ausreichend' (sufficient). NOT in the 'gut' (good, ~2.0–2.5) range many competitive programs implicitly expect.",
    "pattern":"Few programs publish a hard numeric GPA cutoff, but recurring informal benchmarks (TU Dresden ~3.0/4.0, RUB ~3.0/4.0, RPTU ~3.5/4.0) suggest 2.8/4.0 sits at/slightly below the commonly-expected range, especially at TU9 schools (RWTH, KIT, TUM, TU Berlin).",
    "more_lenient_paths":"(1) Universities of Applied Sciences/Hochschulen (RWU, Reutlingen, Furtwangen, Esslingen) — weight practical/industry experience heavily; (2) 'aptitude/suitability assessment' programs rather than hard GPA cutoffs (Saarland, TUM); (3) conditional admission with catch-up modules for near-match degrees (FAU); (4) TU Chemnitz's notably lower B2/IELTS 5.5 bar suggests a less selective overall culture.",
    "weakest_fit":"TUHH Hamburg explicitly states only very-high-GPA applicants are admitted — clearest negative signal found. Use their free pre-check tool first.",
    "strategic_recommendation":"Treat TU9 flagships (TUM, RWTH, KIT, TU Berlin) as reach/stretch applications. Prioritize applied-sciences universities and aptitude-assessment programs (Saarland, TU Chemnitz, RWU, TU Ilmenau) as realistic primary targets. Lean heavily on 2 years of UVM/RTL verification work experience in your SOP — several programs use holistic review where strong experience compensates for a below-average GPA."
  },
  "uni_assist_info": {
    "what":"uni-assist e.V. is a centralized non-profit service many German universities use to pre-check international applicants' documents (degree equivalence, grade conversion, completeness) before forwarding to the university, which makes the final decision.",
    "fee":"EUR 75 for first program application per semester cycle; EUR 30 for each additional program in the same cycle (verify current figures on uni-assist.de).",
    "process":"Create 'My assist' account → upload certified docs/language certs → pay fee → uni-assist evaluates & forwards a VPD to the university. Adds ~4–6 weeks to your timeline — apply well before the university deadline, not uni-assist's.",
    "not_universal":"Not all universities use uni-assist — several TU9 schools and most Hochschulen run admissions in-house via direct portals. Check each program's page."
  },
  "daad_search_coverage_note": "A second, exhaustive research pass (2026-11-08) specifically targeted DAAD's full International Programmes database (https://www2.daad.de/deutschland/studienangebote/international-programmes/en/) to make sure nothing relevant was missed beyond the first hand-picked university list. IMPORTANT LIMITATION: DAAD's database filter UI is JavaScript-driven and could not be directly operated by the research tool — instead, ~18 targeted site:daad.de searches (Microelectronics, Embedded Systems, VLSI, Nanoelectronics, Integrated Circuit, Computer Engineering, Mechatronics, Semiconductor, Electronics, Communications Engineering, Signal Processing, Power Electronics, plus many individual university-name searches) were run and cross-checked against the myguide.de mirror (which uses the same DAAD detail-page IDs). This found 18 new programs not caught the first time, but it is still NOT a guaranteed 100% complete crawl — a few smaller Hochschulen (Wuppertal, Hamm-Lippstadt, Südwestfalen, Amberg-Weiden, Nuremberg Tech, Munich UAS, Munich Ingolstadt, Aschaffenburg, Coburg, Deggendorf, Hof, Landshut, Ansbach, Wismar, Rosenheim) were searched for but returned no confirmed DAAD listing — this does NOT prove they have none, only that this search pass didn't surface any. For true 100% certainty, someone would need to manually operate DAAD's live filter (subject=Electrical Engineering/Engineering general, degree=Master, country=Germany) in a real browser.",
